    "validation_file": {
      "id": "2",
      "name": "test.py",
      "contents": "import re\nfrom unittest import TestCase\nfrom unittest_runner import ValidationProtocol\n\n\ndef read_code():\n    with open('main.py') as main_file:\n        return main_file.read()\n\n\ndef comment_text(code, n):\n    match = re.search(rf'^#\\s*Check {n}[^:\\n]*:[ \\t]*(.+)$', code, re.MULTILINE)\n    return match.group(1).strip() if match else \"\"\n\n\nclass TestInvestigate(TestCase):\n    @classmethod\n    def setUpClass(cls):\n        \"\"\"Run the student's code once and save the results\"\"\"\n        cls.log = ValidationProtocol().get_neighborhood_log()\n        cls.painter = cls.log.painter_logs[0] if cls.log.painter_logs else None\n\n    def test_1_check_1_comment(self):\n        \"\"\"You finished the comment on Check 1\"\"\"\n        if len(comment_text(read_code(), 1)) < 10:\n            self.fail(\"Finish the comment on Check 1. Type after the colon, and name the if keyword, the condition, and the indented block.\")\n\n    def test_2_check_2_comment(self):\n        \"\"\"You finished the comment on Check 2\"\"\"\n        if len(comment_text(read_code(), 2)) < 10:\n            self.fail(\"Finish the comment on Check 2. Type after the colon: where is the Painter, and is the square ahead open?\")\n\n    def test_3_both_checks_are_there(self):\n        \"\"\"Both checks are still in your program\"\"\"\n        if len(re.findall(r'^\\s*if\\s+can_move\\s*\\(\\s*\\)\\s*:', read_code(), re.MULTILINE)) < 2:\n            self.fail(\"Put the extra changes back. Your program should have both if can_move(): checks from the starter code.\")\n\n    def test_4_runs(self):\n        \"\"\"Your program runs\"\"\"\n        if self.painter is None:\n            self.fail(\"Your code didn't run far enough to check. Make sure the setup is still there and that each if line ends with a colon.\")",
      "folderId": "0",
      "active": false,
      "type": "validation"
    },
    "teacher_markdown": "Suggested experiments and what they reveal:\r\n\r\n- Delete one setup move(): the Painter starts both checks one square earlier. Check 1 and Check 2 are both True, so \"I moved!\" prints twice. The code is the same and the answer is different, because the Painter is in a different place.\r\n- Delete two setup moves: both checks are still True, and \"I moved!\" prints twice. The Painter ends two squares from the taxi.\r\n- Add a third copy of Check 1: nothing changes. At the end of the street the check is False, so the block is skipped. Skipped code is not an error.\r\n\r\nMisconceptions to watch for:\r\n\r\n- Expecting identical conditions to always give the same answer. The Painter's position changes between the checks, so the same question can be True and then False.\r\n- Expecting a skipped block to cause an error, or to print something.\r\n- Thinking the print line always runs. It is indented under the if, so it only runs when the condition is True.\r\n\r\nTarget answers for the comments:\r\n\r\n- Check 2: \"The Painter is at the end of the street, so the square ahead is not open.\"\r\n- Check 1: \"The if keyword starts the check, the condition can_move() asks whether the square ahead is open, and the indented block runs only when it is True.\"\r\n\r\nValidation checks that both comments are finished and that both checks are still in the program."
  },
